window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2026-06-2", "source": "2026年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on future applications of robots on campus. You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but not more than 180 words.", "sample": "Robots are no longer figures from science fiction; in the near future, they are likely to become common helpers\non university campuses. As the university newspaper invites students to imagine future applications of robots\non campus, I believe intelligent machines will mainly reshape our campus life in three areas: library services,\nlaboratory assistance, and daily guidance.\nFirst, in the library, robots could locate, fetch, and even deliver requested books to reading rooms, saving\nstudents valuable study time. Second, in science labs, robotic assistants might handle repetitive experiments or\ndangerous chemicals, allowing students to focus on analysis and innovation. Third, robots placed around the\ncampus could provide real-time directions and campus information for visitors and freshmen, reducing\nconfusion during busy periods.\nOf course, these technologies should serve people rather than replace human interaction. If universities\nintroduce robots wisely, campus life will become more efficient, safe, and welcoming."}, "translation": {"prompt": "餐桌礼仪 (dining etiquette) 是中华传统文化的重要组成部分，蕴含着热情有礼的待客之道。为了招待客人，主人会根据客人的口味和喜好准备丰富精致的菜肴。就餐座位的安排非常讲究，主人坐在正对大门的位置，在主人身边就座的是最重要的客人。进餐时，主人会不时地为客人添酒加菜。这种独特的餐桌礼仪已延续了数千年，对促进和谐的人际交往起到了重要的作用。", "sample": "Dining etiquette is an important part of traditional Chinese culture, embodying the warm and courteous way of treating guests. To entertain guests, the host prepares rich and exquisite dishes according to their tastes and preferences. The seating arrangement is very particular: the host sits in the seat directly facing the gate, while the most important guest sits beside him. During the meal, he refills wine and adds more dishes for the guests from time to time. This unique dining etiquette has lasted for thousands of years and has played an important role in promoting harmonious interpersonal relationships."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2026-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "It may sound surprising, but people who are supersensitive to coffee's bitter taste actually drink more of it, a new study finds.\nThis sensitivity isn't  __26__  a matter of taste, either, but rather is influenced by a person's genetic makeup, the researchers said in the study.\n\"You'd expect that people who are particularly sensitive to the bitter taste of caffeine would drink less coffee,\" said study senior researcher Marilyn Cornelis. \"The  __27__  results of our study suggest coffee consumers acquire a taste for or an ability to detect the bitterness of caffeine due to the learned positive  __28__  brought out by caffeine.\"\nPut another way, people who have a  __29__  ability to taste the bitterness of coffee learn to associate \"good things with it,\" Cornelis said. This finding is surprising, given that bitterness often serves as a warning mechanism to  __30__  people to spit out harmful substances, the scientists said.\nResearchers conducted the study to understand how genetics influences people's  __31__  of tea, coffee and alcohol which tend to taste bitter, said lead researcher Jue Sheng Ong.\n\"While all bitter flavors may seem the same, we  __32__  the bitterness of Brussels sprouts(抱子甘蓝), quinine(奎宁) and caffeine  __33__  ,\" Ong told Live Science. \"The degree to which we find these flavors bitter is, in part, determined by your genes.\"\nPeople with the genes to taste the bitterness of green vegetables are more likely to prefer tea to coffee. In  __34__  , people who were more sensitive to quinine's bitter flavors and those found in green vegetables tended to  __35__  coffee.", "matchIntro": "Exercise and Dementia", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "If this doesn't encourage you to squeeze in a workout today, nothing will: a new study from Sweden shows that women who were highly fit in mid-life were nearly 90% less likely to get dementia(失智) decades later."}, {"label": "B", "text": "After initial exercise tests in middle age, researchers followed the women for 44 years. Both groups lived just as long, but those who could ride an exercise bike at a fast rate for 6 minutes in the initial test had a much lower risk of dementia later on than those who couldn't complete the workout."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The study, published Wednesday in the journal Neurology, couldn't prove that exercise prevented dementia, and the findings aren't a surprise—it's long been known there's a correlation between exercise and decreased dementia risk—but the results were particularly dramatic. About 5% of the women with the highest peak workload—those who were able to bike the hardest over those 6 minutes—developed dementia, compared to 25% of those with medium fitness and 45% who weren't fit enough to finish the test, the study found. Overall, women who were highly fit compared to those who were moderately fit decreased their risk of dementia by 88%. The few highly fit women who did develop dementia became symptomatic at age 90 on average, 11 years later than the moderately fit."}, {"label": "D", "text": "\"I'm very surprised that the finding was so strong,\" said Ingmar Skoog, the paper's senior author and a psychiatry(精神病学) professor at The University of Gothenburg in Sweden. \"It really shows the importance of exercise.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "Dementia, as defined in medical dictionaries, is a broad term that describes a loss of thinking ability, memory, attention, logical reasoning, and other mental abilities. These changes are severe enough to interfere with social or occupational functioning. Dementia isn't a disease. Instead, it's a group of symptoms caused by other conditions. It happens when the parts of your brain used for learning, memory, decision making, and language are damaged or diseased. About 5%-8% of adults over age 65 have some form of dementia. This percentage doubles every 5 years after 65. As many as half of people in their 80s have some form of dementia."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Dementia is not temporary confusion or forgetfulness that might result from an infection that heals without treatment. It can also come from an underlying(潜在的) illness or side effects of medications. Dementia typically gets worse over time. People with dementia have problems with thinking and remembering that affect their ability to manage their daily life."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Dementia is typically associated with declines in cognitive function. Yet researchers have found an association between dementia and declines in hearing and other basic sensory functions."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Though the study mentioned above was fairly small (only 191 women took the initial fitness test, which means it's hard to maintain statistical significance while breaking the group down into sub-categories of more or less fit), it does give us hope that dementia can be prevented or delayed through exercise in one's middle age."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Alzheimer's(阿尔茨海默症) and other dementias are believed to begin 15-20 years before symptoms even appear, so it makes sense that exercising in mid-life would affect the risk, Skoog said. Exercise alone is not likely to prevent Alzheimer's, but the study shows people are not helpless in the face of one of the most feared, costliest and common diseases of old age, he added. And the same activities that help prevent Alzheimer's—including avoiding smoking, getting adequate exercise and sleep and eating a healthy diet—also prevent heart disease, he said, making them even more worthwhile. \"You can do something yourself to decrease your odds,\" Skoog said."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Maintaining a healthy lifestyle in mid-life, decades before disease sets in, makes sense, said David Knopman, a fellow of the American Academy of Neurology, who was not involved in the study. \"I suspect it's a dose,\" said Knopman, also associate director of the Alzheimer's Disease Research Center at Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota. \"Starting in late life is better than not starting at all, but starting in mid-life seems to offer a larger benefit.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Although it is not entirely clear why exercise helps put off or prevent Alzheimer's, Knopman said it's likely that exercise maintains good blood flow to the brain. \"When the brain is healthier from a vascular(血管的) point of view, it can absorb more Alzheimer's pathogen(病原体) before people become symptomatic,\" he said."}, {"label": "L", "text": "The message isn't that everyone needs to run marathons in middle age, Knopman said, but a healthy lifestyle pays off."}, {"label": "M", "text": "This type of study can't say exactly what kind of exercise is best, or how much is needed—only a study that has a placebo(安慰剂) group and tracks people going forward can do that, said Keith Fargo, director of scientific programs and outreach at the non-profit Alzheimer's Association. \"The literature has not yet settled on an amount or type of exercise that is going to be key, although the bulk of literature has suggested that aerobic(有氧的) exercise is what you need to be doing,\" he said. That doesn't mean you have to compete in marathons but \"more than a 10-min dog walk\" would be a good idea, Fargo said."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Several lifestyle studies are getting underway soon, including one backed by the Alzheimer's Association called the US POINTER study, which will offer at-risk adults, ages 60-79, a series of lifestyle interventions(干预) to see if they impact Alzheimer's risk. That and other Alzheimer's-related studies are always looking for volunteers to participate, he said."}, {"label": "O", "text": "In the next three to five years, these studies should allow researchers to provide clearer recommendations for exercise and other lifestyle modifications that might reduce Alzheimer's risk, such as sleep, diet and social activities, Fargo said. But it's already quite clear that exercising at any point in life is better for your brain than not exercising at all, Fargo added."}, {"label": "P", "text": "\"If you don't want to have dementia when you're 80, the time to start getting fit is now,\" he said. \"It may not necessarily give you a longer life, but there's a compelling body of work that it will give you more good years.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. Those people live in constant fear that their \"deception\" will be exposed.\nIt is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well.\nThe study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression.", "47": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. Those people live in constant fear that their \"deception\" will be exposed.\nIt is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well.\nThe study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression.", "48": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. Those people live in constant fear that their \"deception\" will be exposed.\nIt is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well.\nThe study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression.", "49": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. Those people live in constant fear that their \"deception\" will be exposed.\nIt is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well.\nThe study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression.", "50": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. Those people live in constant fear that their \"deception\" will be exposed.\nIt is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well.\nThe study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression.", "51": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; \"penta-\" meaning 5, and \"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual(感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics(美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us.", "52": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; \"penta-\" meaning 5, and \"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual(感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics(美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us.", "53": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; \"penta-\" meaning 5, and \"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual(感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics(美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us.", "54": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; \"penta-\" meaning 5, and \"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual(感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics(美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us.", "55": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; \"penta-\" meaning 5, and \"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual(感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics(美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us."}, "questions": [{"id": "2026-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the news report say about Nancy Gifford?", "options": ["A) She was born months after the end of World War I.", "B) She has been living in her house throughout her life.", "C) She suffered a great deal during the Second World War.", "D) She is a 102-year-old woman living in Somerset Village."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She was born months after the end of World War I.\n【定位】Nancy Gifford was born months after the end of the First World War, and spent 102 years\nliving in her three-bedroom house in the Somerset Village of Street.\n南希·吉福德出生于第一次世界大战结束数月后，并在萨默塞特郡斯特里特村的一栋三居室房\n屋里住了102年。\n【信号】首段人物定义句。新闻首段在抛出事件（104岁老妇卖房）后，用 Nancy Gifford was\nborn months after the end of the First World War 给出人物关键身份信息，直接回应题\n干所问。\n【替换】Nancy Gifford was born months after the end of the First World War ↔\nShe was born months after the end of World War I.\n同义替换：the First World War = World War I\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文说 the house she has lived in since she was two years old，并非一生都在此居住；\nShe has been living in her house throughout her life. 把起点从两岁偷换为'一生'。\n借用原文：the house she has lived in since she was two years old\nC) 过度推断\n原文仅说 having lived in it through the Second World War，表示她经历了二战，但并\n未说她 suffered a great deal；She suffered a great deal during the Second World\nWar. 把'经历'强改为'遭受巨大痛苦'。\n借用原文：having lived in it through the Second World War\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说 Nancy Gifford 是 A 104-year-old woman，且 spent 102 years living in her\nthree-bedroom house in the Somerset Village of Street；She is a 102-year-old\nwoman living in Somerset Village. 把居住时长误植为年龄，同时把 Somerset Village of\nStreet 简化为 Somerset Village。\n借用原文：A 104-year-old woman", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "Why is Mrs. Gifford selling the house?", "options": ["A) She has been persuaded by the estate agent.", "B) She needs the money badly for her health.", "C) She is leaving Glastonbury Village.", "D) She is moving to a nursing home."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is moving to a nursing home.\n【定位】Mrs. Gifford is selling the house and moving to a nursing home in nearby Glastonbury\nbecause of her health.\n吉福德夫人因为健康原因，正在出售房屋并搬到附近格拉斯顿伯里的一家养老院。\n【信号】because of 因果信号。答案句以 because of her health 结尾，直接回答 Why；卖房与搬\n去养老院是并列结果，健康才是原因。\n【替换】Mrs. Gifford is selling the house and moving to a nursing home in nearby Glastonbury because of\nher health\n↔ She is moving to a nursing home.\n因果同构：卖房是为搬去养老院\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文 According to the estate agent, the house was built in 1882... 只说明中介提供了房\n屋信息，并未说 Mrs. Gifford 是被中介说服卖房；She has been persuaded by the\nestate agent. 把'中介存在'曲解为'被中介说服'。\n借用原文：According to the estate agent\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 because of her health 说明健康是原因，但并未说她 needs the money badly；She\nneeds the money badly for her health. 把'因健康搬家'偷换成'因健康急需钱'。\n借用原文：because of her health\nC) 与原文相反\n原文说 moving to a nursing home in nearby Glastonbury，即她要搬去格拉斯顿伯里；\nShe is leaving Glastonbury Village. 把'搬入附近格拉斯顿伯里'说成'离开格拉斯顿伯里'，\n方向相反。\n借用原文：moving to a nursing home in nearby Glastonbury\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 The navy rescued a man who survived 24 days floating in the sea on a boat by eating tomato sauce\nand vegetable powder. The man, 47, wrote the word \"HELP\" in English on the boat, which was key to\nhis rescue.\n海军救起了一名男子，他靠食用番茄酱和蔬菜粉，在一艘船上漂浮海上生存了24天。这名47岁的男子在\n船上用英语写下了“HELP”一词，这对他的获救起到了关键作用。\n2 The man told authorities that his experience began in December when currents swept the sailboat out\nto sea while he was making repairs. To survive, the man collected rainwater with a cloth. He had to\nconstantly remove water from the boat to prevent it from sinking. He also tried to light a fire to send a\ndistress signal without success. Finally, a plane passed by and he signaled with a mirror. He said\nthe navy told him that he was spotted when the plane passed again. The sailboat was abandoned at\nsea when the man was picked up by a nearby merchant ship. He was found in good health but had\nlost weight.\n这名男子告诉当局，他的遭遇始于12月，当时他正在维修帆船，洋流将帆船冲向了大海。为了生存，他用\n一块布收集雨水。他不得不不断将船里的水排出去，以防船只沉没。他还试图生火发出求救信号，但没有\n成功。最后，一架飞机经过时，他用镜子发出了信号。他说，海军告诉他，飞机再次经过时发现了他。这\n名男子被附近的一艘商船救起时，帆船已被遗弃在海上。他被发现时身体状况良好，但体重有所下降。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the news report say was key to the man's rescue?", "options": ["A) The navy's timely arrival.", "B) His rich sailing experience.", "C) The word HELP written on the boat.", "D) His use of a mirror to send a signal."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The word HELP written on the boat.\n【定位】The man, 47, wrote the word \"HELP\" in English on the boat, which was key to his rescue.\n这名47岁的男子在船身上用英文写下了\"HELP\"（救命）一词，而这正是他获救的关键。\n【信号】非限制性定语从句 'which was key to his rescue' 直接点题。which was key to his\nrescue 把前文的 'wrote the word \"HELP\" in English on the boat' 明确标注为获救关键，\n是新闻中少见的显性答案标记。\n【替换】wrote the word \"HELP\" in English on the boat ↔ The word HELP written on the boat\n动作名词化 + 核心信息保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThe navy's timely arrival 中的 navy 确实出现在录音中，但原文只说 The navy rescued a\nman，海军是执行救援的一方，而非 'key to his rescue' 所指的原因。\n借用原文：The navy rescued a man\nB) 未提及\nHis rich sailing experience 中的 sailing experience 在整段录音中没有任何对应信息，属\n于无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\nHis use of a mirror to send a signal 中的 mirror 确实在 he signaled with a mirror 中出\n现，但这是男子在飞机经过时的求救尝试；原文明确说 key 是 HELP，镜子只是另一条求生\n手段。\n借用原文：he signaled with a mirror", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What do we learn about the man when he was rescued?", "options": ["A) He lost all consciousness.", "B) He was in good condition.", "C) He was too distressed to pick himself up.", "D) He entirely abandoned himself to despair."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He was in good condition.\n【定位】He was found in good health but had lost weight.\n他被发现时身体状况良好，但体重减轻了。\n【信号】新闻结尾总结人物状态的收束句。He was found in good health 中的 was found 是被救\n后的总结性动词，in good health 直接回应题干 'when he was rescued' 的状态询问。\n【替换】in good health ↔ He was in good condition\n近义形容词替换\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nHe lost all consciousness 中的 lost 可能让考生联想到 had lost weight，但 weight 与\nconsciousness 是完全不同的概念，属于偷换概念。\n借用原文：had lost weight\nC) 未提及\nHe was too distressed to pick himself up 中的 distressed 与 pick himself up 在录音中\n均未出现，且与 'in good health' 的正面评价相反。\nD) 原词陷阱\nHe entirely abandoned himself to despair 中的 abandoned 可能让考生联想到 The\nsailboat was abandoned at sea，但原文主语是 sailboat（船被遗弃），选项却把它改成\nHe abandoned himself（自暴自弃），且 despair 在录音中从未出现。\n借用原文：The sailboat was abandoned at sea", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about the elementary and middle schools to be closed in Denver?", "options": ["A) Their efforts to provide social services have been ignored.", "B) They have been criticized by the Denver school board.", "C) They have taken various steps to get more funding.", "D) Their enrollment has been on a noticeable decline."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Their enrollment has been on a noticeable decline.\n【定位】The board of Denver public schools is set to close several elementary and middle schools to\nmake up for a decreased budget caused by declining enrollment.\n丹佛公立学校董事会准备关闭几所中小学，以弥补因入学人数下降而造成的预算减少。\n【信号】因果信号 caused by。答案句中 caused by declining enrollment 直接给出关闭学校的\ncausal chain：预算减少 ← 入学下降 ← 学校关闭。\n【替换】declining enrollment ↔ Their enrollment has been on a noticeable decline.\n同义替换 declining = on a noticeable decline\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 A 说‘提供社会服务的努力被忽视’，但录音末句只说 cannot provide the same\nmental health supports, social and emotional services，是预算不足导致无法提供，并\n非努力被忽视；social services 这个词录音确实念到了，但语义与选项不符。\n借用原文：social and emotional services\nB) 未提及\n选项 B 说学校被校董会批评，但录音中校董会是作出关闭决定的主体，并未出现 criticized\n的语义，属于无中生有。\nC)\n选项 C 说学校采取各种措施争取资金，但录音讲的是‘因预算减少而关校’，没有提到学校主\n动 steps to get more funding，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the Denver district expect to do in the next three years?", "options": ["A) Approve criteria for closing schools with decreased funding.", "B) Enroll 3,000 fewer elementary and middle school students.", "C) Decide which elementary and middle schools to close.", "D) Alter its per-pupil funding policy on a rational basis."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Enroll 3,000 fewer elementary and middle school students.\n【定位】In the next three years, the district expects to enroll 3,000 fewer elementary and middle\nschool students, it said.\n该地区表示，在未来三年内，预计中小学入学人数将减少3000人。\n【信号】时间信号 + 数字信号。答案句中 In the next three years 与 expects to enroll 3,000\nfewer 形成时间与数字的双重信号，直接对应题干。\n【替换】enroll 3,000 fewer elementary and middle school students ↔\nEnroll 3,000 fewer elementary and middle school students.\n原词复现\n【排除】A)\n选项 A 说‘批准关闭标准’，但录音中 The school board approved criteria 用一般过去时，\n表示已经批准，不是 expect to do 的未来动作。\n借用原文：The school board approved criteria\nC) 答非所问\n选项 C 说‘决定关闭哪些中小学’，但录音中 The specific schools will be announced in\nthe coming days 指近期宣布，不是 in the next three years 的预期动作；属于答非所\n问。\n借用原文：The specific schools will be announced in the coming days\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 D 说‘改变 per-pupil funding 政策’，但录音只是用 per-pupil funding 解释预算计算\n方式，并未说要 alter policy，属于偷换概念。\n借用原文：per-pupil funding", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why are some schools in Denver to be closed according to the district spokesperson?", "options": ["A) To enable the district to remain competitive.", "B) To avoid a negative impact on its economy.", "C) To keep the district free of debt.", "D) To obtain the expected funding."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To keep the district free of debt.\n【定位】\"We know that there are impacts and it's heartbreaking and difficult work. But in order for the\ndistrict to stay out of debt, it's a step that needs to be taken,\" he said.\n‘我们知道这会带来影响，这是令人心碎且艰难的工作。但为了让学区不负债，这是必须采取\n的一步，’他说。\n【信号】目的信号 in order for... to stay out of debt。发言人先用 But 转折，再用 in order\nfor the district to stay out of debt 点明关闭学校的根本目的，答案落点在 stay out\nof debt。\n【替换】stay out of debt ↔ To keep the district free of debt.\n同义替换 stay out of = keep... free of\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 A 说‘让学区保持竞争力’，但录音全文未出现 competitive / competition 等词，属于\n无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 B 说‘避免对经济造成负面影响’，但录音中 there are impacts 是承认影响存在，并未\n说 avoid negative impact on its economy；impact 原词出现，但语义被扭曲。\n借用原文：there are impacts\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 D 说‘为了获得预期资金’，但录音讲的是预算减少、按 per-pupil funding 计算缺口，\n关闭学校是为了 stay out of debt（减少支出/避免负债），而不是 obtain funding（获得\n资金）；funding 原词出现，但目的被替换。\n借用原文：decreased budget", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why does the woman call the man?", "options": ["A) To book tickets for a musical.", "B) To enquire about the price of cats.", "C) To pay for her afternoon flight to London.", "D) To have her personal computer repaired."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To book tickets for a musical.\n【定位】I've been trying to book tickets for the musical Cats in London on the evening of May the\n28th, but I can't get the computer to accept my card payment.\n我一直在尝试预订5月28日晚上伦敦音乐剧《猫》的门票，但电脑不接受我的银行卡支付。\n【信号】被问者直接说明来电目的。女士在第二段直接说 I've been trying to book tickets for the\nmusical Cats，这是对话开头最典型的“说明来意”句型，答案落在此处。\n【替换】book tickets for the musical Cats ↔ To book tickets for a musical.\n同义替换：具体剧名概括为 musical\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 B 的 cats 盗用了录音原词 Cats，但录音中 Cats 是音乐剧名，不是宠物猫，更不存在\n“询问猫的价格”。\n借用原文：the musical Cats\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 C 的 London 和 afternoon 都是录音里出现的词（in London 与问候语 good\nafternoon），但录音说的是伦敦的演出，不是下午的航班，属于把地点词和时间词张冠李\n戴。\n借用原文：in London\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 D 的 computer 盗用了 the computer to accept my card payment，但女士说电脑\n只是不接受支付，并没有说要修电脑，核心目的仍是订票。\n借用原文：I can't get the computer to accept my card payment", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman's husband?", "options": ["A) He frequents the Lion Theatre.", "B) He knows how to sell things well.", "C) He is a computer science expert.", "D) He is away on a business trip."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He is away on a business trip.\n【定位】My husband usually does all of these things, but he is in Italy on a business trip until next\nThursday, and I know how quickly these things can sell out.\n我丈夫通常做这些事，但他现在在意大利出差，要到下周四才回来，而且我知道这些票卖得\n很快。\n【信号】转折 but 后出关键信息。女士说 My husband usually does all of these things, but...，\n转折词 but 之后 usually 所暗示的“常规情况”被颠覆，真正要考的丈夫当前状态落在 but\n后面。\n【替换】he is in Italy on a business trip ↔ He is away on a business trip.\n同义替换：具体地点概括为 away\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 A 的 Lion Theatre 盗用了 the Lion Theatre in London，但那是 Cats 演出的剧院，\n与丈夫是否常去无关。\n借用原文：the Lion Theatre in London\nB) 未提及 选项 B 的 sell things well 在录音中没有对应表述，属于无中生有。\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 C 的 computer 盗用了 the computer 与 I'm not very good with computers，但录\n音只说女士自己不擅长电脑，并未说丈夫是电脑专家。\n借用原文：I'm not very good with computers", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man say about the tickets at the front?", "options": ["A) They are sold at a discount.", "B) They are in low demand.", "C) They are no longer available.", "D) They are not too expensive."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They are no longer available.\n【定位】Actually, all the tickets at the front are already sold out anyway.\n其实，前排的票反正都已经卖完了。\n【信号】Actually 引出事实修正。男士先用 But I can offer you two upstairs near the middle 给\n出替代方案，随后用 actually 进一步说明原因——前排票已售罄，答案句就在 actually\n之后。\n【替换】all the tickets at the front are already sold out ↔ They are no longer available.\n同义替换：sold out = no longer available\n【排除】A) 未提及 选项 A 的 discount 在录音中没有对应信息，属于无中生有。\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 B 说“需求低”，但录音说 all the tickets at the front are already sold out，说明需求\n很高，选项与原文相反。\n借用原文：all the tickets at the front are already sold out anyway\nD) 未提及\n选项 D 的 not too expensive 涉及价格评价，但录音中完全没有讨论票价高低。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the woman say she is scared of?", "options": ["A) Heights.", "B) Circus.", "C) Wild animals.", "D) Dark nights."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Heights.\n【定位】I'm scared of heights, and you can never see anything if you are too high up anyway.\n我恐高，而且坐得太高的话根本什么都看不见。\n【信号】直接陈述 scared of。女士说 I'm scared of heights， scared of 是题目关键词的直接复\n现，答案紧随其后。\n【替换】I'm scared of heights ↔ Heights.\n原词复现\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 B 的 Circus 与音乐剧主题相近，但录音中没有提到马戏团，属于利用话题场制造的无\n中生有干扰。\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 C 的 Wild animals 盗用了音乐剧名 Cats 给人的动物联想，但录音根本没有提到野生\n动物。\n借用原文：the musical Cats\nD) 未提及\n选项 D 的 Dark nights 在录音中没有对应内容，属于常见恐惧意象的凭空设置。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why hasn't the man answered the woman's text messages lately?", "options": ["A) He finds it bothersome to reply.", "B) He has switched off his phone.", "C) He hasn't got the time to text back.", "D) He doesn't know how to respond."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He has switched off his phone.\n【定位】Oh, I didn't know you had sent any. I turned off my phone almost a week ago and haven't\nturned it back on since then.\n哦，我不知道你发了短信。我大概一周前关掉了手机，之后就没再打开过。\n【信号】直接应答 + 关键动作。女人一开口就问为何没回短信，男人用 I turned off my phone\nalmost a week ago and haven't turned it back on since then. 直接给出原因，这是对话\n中被问者的核心应答句。\n【替换】I turned off my phone almost a week ago and haven't turned it back on since then. ↔\nHe has switched off his phone.\n同义词替换（turn off = switch off）\n【排除】A) 未提及 男人从未表达回复短信令他厌烦，属于无中生有。\nC) 未提及\n录音中男人说的是『我不知道你发了短信』和『关机』，没有提到没时间回短信。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说 I didn't know you had sent any.，是『不知道你发了短信』，选项 He doesn't\nknow how to respond. 把不知对象偷换成『不知如何回应』。\n借用原文：I didn't know you had sent any.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What was the man shocked to realize after he downloaded the APP?", "options": ["A) His lack of sufficient sleep at night.", "B) His addiction to mobile phone games.", "C) His excessive use of the mobile phone.", "D) His high frequency of surfing the net."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） His excessive use of the mobile phone.\n【定位】Well, I downloaded an APP that recorded how much time I spent on my phone. And I was\nshocked to realize I was using my phone an average of 8 hours a day, and on my worst day, I\nused it for 12 hours.\n嗯，我下载了一个记录手机使用时长的APP。我震惊地发现我平均每天用手机8小时，最多\n的一天用了12小时。\n【信号】题干关键词原词复现 + 数据强调。男人的话里出现 shocked to realize，与题干 shocked\nto realize 原词呼应，后面 8 hours a day、12 hours 用具体数字凸显『使用过度』。\n【替换】I was using my phone an average of 8 hours a day, and on my worst day, I used it for 12 hours. ↔\nHis excessive use of the mobile phone.\n数字概括为『过度使用』\nrecorded how much time I spent on my phone ↔ His excessive use of the mobile phone.\n时间记录映射到使用强度\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n女人提到自己的朋友玩手机游戏『all day and into the night』，这说的是朋友，不是男人\n的情况。\n借用原文：he sometimes played them all day and into the night.\nB) 原词陷阱\n女人问 Were you addicted to mobile phone games?，男人回答 No, my problem was I\nwas spending too much time on social media...，选项 His addiction to mobile phone\ngames. 直接盗用问句原词，却被明确否定。\n借用原文：Were you addicted to mobile phone games?\nD) 未提及\n录音只提到 social media / messaging / commenting，没有说 surfing the net，属于用\n近义概念捏造。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What did the man spend much time doing with his phone?", "options": ["A) Shopping online and paying for his purchases.", "B) Communicating with friends on social media.", "C) Exploring whatever content to his liking.", "D) Sharing and posting pictures and videos."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Communicating with friends on social media.\n【定位】No, my problem was I was spending too much time on social media, messaging friends and\ncommenting on other people's posts.\n不，我的问题是我花了太多时间在社交媒体上，给朋友发消息、评论别人的帖子。\n【信号】否定后的真正落点。女人先猜测是游戏，男人用 No, my problem was I was spending\ntoo much time on social media, messaging friends and commenting on other\npeople's posts. 纠正并给出真实答案，否定词 No 后面才是考点。\n【替换】spending too much time on social media, messaging friends ↔\nCommunicating with friends on social media.\n动名词短语同义替换\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 Shopping online and paying for his purchases. 来自女人说的 shopping online\nand paying for everything with my phone，不是男人的行为。\n借用原文：shopping online and paying for everything with my phone.\nC) 未提及 录音没有提到 exploring content / whatever to his liking。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说 commenting on other people's posts，选项 Sharing and posting pictures and\nvideos. 添加了 pictures and videos，并把 commenting 换成 sharing，属于偷换概念。\n借用原文：commenting on other people's posts.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "Why does the woman say she has to use her mobile phone?", "options": ["A) She prefers chatting with others online to meeting them.", "B) There are few physical stores near where she lives.", "C) Ordering meals online is a great convenience to her.", "D) She needs to talk with her friends and family far away."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She needs to talk with her friends and family far away.\n【定位】That's great for you, but my friends and family don't live in this city, so we can't see each\nother often, and I don't have any choice but to chat with them online.\n这对你很好，但我的朋友和家人不住在这个城市，所以我们不能经常见面，我别无选择，只\n能和他们在线聊天。\n【信号】but 后的不得已理由。女人先肯定男人的做法，随即用 but my friends and family don't\nlive in this city... I don't have any choice but to chat with them online 给出自己的难\n处，but 与 have no choice but to 共同标出答案落点。\n【替换】my friends and family don't live in this city, so we can't see each other often ↔\nShe needs to talk with her friends and family far away.\n『不住同城』替换为『far away』\nI don't have any choice but to chat with them online ↔\nShe needs to talk with her friends and family far away.\n『不得不聊』对应『需要聊』\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说 I don't have any choice but to chat with them online，是客观无奈；选项 She\nprefers chatting with others online to meeting them. 却说她更喜欢线上聊天，改变了\n语义极性。\n借用原文：I don't have any choice but to chat with them online.\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 There are few physical stores near where she lives. 借用了男人说的 I go to\nphysical stores and pay in cash.，但说话人与话题都不属于女人。\n借用原文：I go to physical stores and pay in cash.\nC) 答非所问\n录音中女人提到的是 shopping online / paying for everything，没有提到 ordering\nmeals online，且这也不是她必须使用手机的核心理由。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the passage say about the gap in wage gains between job switchers and those who stay in their jobs?", "options": ["A) It is now narrowing.", "B) It keeps increasing.", "C) It has remained huge.", "D) It will soon disappear."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is now narrowing.\n【定位】But the gap is now closing.\n但这个差距现在正在缩小。\n【信号】转折 but 后直接给出结论。But the gap is now closing. 中 But 承接前一句“跳槽者去年工\n资涨幅更高”，转折后才是当前真实趋势；后文又重复 The gap in wage gains between\npeople who switched jobs and those who stayed in their jobs is now closing.，形成\n强调。\n【替换】the gap is now closing ↔ It is now narrowing.\n同义替换 closing = narrowing\n【排除】B) 与原文相反 录音说差距在 closing，而 It keeps increasing. 说持续扩大，正好相反。\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音提到过去差距通常约 one percentage point，七月扩大到 2.6 percentage points，但\n这些都是铺垫；It has remained huge. 把过去某一时刻的较大差距偷换成“一直很大”，与\nnow closing 矛盾。\n借用原文：In July though, the gap widened to 2.6 percentage points.\nD) 过度推断\nclosing 只表示“缩小”，不等于“完全消失”；It will soon disappear. 把趋势推向极端，录音\n未提。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do we learn about wage growth now?", "options": ["A) It is fluctuating between 5-7%.", "B) It is slowing down for everyone.", "C) It is faster for those who stay in their jobs.", "D) It is inadequate for dealing with inflation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is slowing down for everyone.\n【定位】But now wage growth is slowing down for everyone, and job switchers are getting smaller\nwage bumps in their new jobs.\n但现在所有人的工资增长都在放缓，跳槽者在新工作中获得的工资涨幅也变小了。\n【信号】But now 时间转折 + for everyone 绝对化表述。But now wage growth is slowing\ndown for everyone 中 But now 把话题拉回当下，for everyone 给出全称范围，是细节题\n中最直接的答案信号。\n【替换】wage growth is slowing down for everyone ↔ It is slowing down for everyone.\n原句复现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 7.3% 和 5.4%，而 It is fluctuating between 5-7%. 把这两个数字捏成一个\n“5%-7% 之间波动”的说法，但录音说的是两类人各自的平均涨幅，并非整体在 5-7% 之间\n波动。\n借用原文：those who change jobs get an average 7.3% raise every year, while those who stay get a", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is the labor market likely doing according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Booming.", "B) Stabilizing.", "C) Struggling.", "D) Cooling."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Cooling.\n【定位】The labor market is likely cooling, because even a shortage of workers isn't overcoming the\nhistoric imbalance between employers and employees in the U.S.\n劳动力市场可能在降温，因为即使工人短缺也无法扭转美国雇主与雇员之间长期存在的不\n平衡。\n【信号】结论句 + because 因果解释。The labor market is likely cooling 位于段落中部，是作者\n给出的判断；后接 because 解释原因，进一步确认该句为结论落点。\n【替换】The labor market is likely cooling ↔ Cooling.\n原词复现\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说市场 likely cooling，Booming. 表示繁荣火热，方向完全相反；且录音提到\ndemand for workers has reached or is nearing its peak，暗示上升空间已尽。\n借用原文：The labor market is likely cooling\nB) 偷换概念\n录音说 cooling（降温），Stabilizing. 说稳定，是两个不同的状态；降温意味着从高点回\n落，稳定则意味着保持不变。\nC) 未提及\n录音没有提到 labor market 在 struggling；文中说的是工资增长放缓、市场降温，这是周\n期性描述，并非“ struggling（挣扎）”这种带有困难色彩的评价。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does a new study show about highly processed foods?", "options": ["A) They have smaller amounts of nutrients than unprocessed diets.", "B) Their contribution to calorie consumption is hard to determine.", "C) They account for nearly half of the average American diet.", "D) Their low price and convenience negatively impact health."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Their low price and convenience negatively impact health.\n【定位】But a new study shows that their low price and convenience come at a cost to health.\n但一项新研究表明，它们低廉的价格和便利是以健康为代价的。\n【信号】研究结论信号句。录音用 a new study shows that 引出结论，其后 their low price and\nconvenience come at a cost to health 直接对应选项 D 的核心语义。\n【替换】their low price and convenience come at a cost to health ↔\nTheir low price and convenience negatively impact health.\n同义替换：以健康为代价=对健康有负面影响\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音明确说 both diets had the same amounts of nutrients，即两种饮食营养成分相同；\n选项 A 却说 smaller amounts of nutrients，与原文相反。\n借用原文：both diets had the same amounts of nutrients\nB) 未提及\n录音只提到 they consumed about 500 more calories per day，给出了具体数字，从未\n说 contribution to calorie consumption is hard to determine。\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说 make up as much as 60% of the average American diet，而 nearly half 约等于\n50%，数字被偷换；同时它用了 average American diet 这一原词来增强诱惑力。\n借用原文：make up as much as 60% of the average American diet", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What are highly processed foods linked to, according to other research?", "options": ["A) A strong desire to overeat.", "B) A lack of nutrients like fiber.", "C) A higher risk of cancer and obesity.", "D) A greater intake of sugar and salt."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A higher risk of cancer and obesity.\n【定位】It wasn't a shock to find highly processed foods weren't healthy; other research has linked\nthem to a higher risk of cancer and obesity.\n发现加工食品不健康并不令人惊讶；其他研究已把它们与更高的癌症和肥胖风险联系起来。\n【信号】其他研究关联句。录音中 other research has linked them to 是明显的考点信号，后面的\na higher risk of cancer and obesity 即为问题所问的关联内容。\n【替换】a higher risk of cancer and obesity ↔ A higher risk of cancer and obesity.\n关键短语直接复现\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音先说 other research has linked them to a higher risk of cancer and obesity，随后\n才讲新试验发现 'causes people to overeat'；选项 A 把 'overeating' 这个结果张冠李戴到\nother research 上。\n借用原文：causes people to overeat\nB) 与原文相反\n录音 both diets had the same amounts of nutrients, like sugar, fat, fiber and salt 说明\n营养成分含量相同，选项 B 却说 A lack of nutrients like fiber，与原文相反。\n借用原文：both diets had the same amounts of nutrients, like sugar, fat, fiber and salt\nD) 偷换概念\n录音 sugar, fat and salt didn't seem to be what caused people to overeat 表明糖、脂\n肪、盐并非导致暴食的原因；选项 D 却说 'A greater intake of sugar and salt'，把原文的\n否定/排除偷换成了肯定的健康风险关联。\n借用原文：sugar, fat and salt didn't seem to be what caused people to overeat", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do people tend to do when eating highly processed foods?", "options": ["A) Eat more quickly.", "B) Feel full sooner.", "C) Chew thoroughly.", "D) Swallow bit by bit."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Eat more quickly.\n【定位】The study also found that, when eating a highly processed diet, people ate faster.\n研究还发现，当人们吃高度加工的饮食时，他们吃得更快。\n【信号】研究发现的行为结果。录音用 The study also found that 引出另一项发现，其后 people\nate faster 直接回答题干所问的行为。\n【替换】people ate faster ↔ Eat more quickly.\n同义替换：ate faster = eat more quickly\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音 you're not giving your guts enough time to signal to your brain that you've had\nenough calories and that you're full 说明大脑更晚收到饱腹信号；选项 B 说 Feel full\nsooner，与原文相反。\n借用原文：you're not giving your guts enough time to signal to your brain that you've had enough\ncalories and that you're full\nC) 偷换概念\n录音 Highly processed food tends to be softer, which makes it easier to chew and\nswallow 只说更容易咀嚼吞咽，并未说人们会 chew thoroughly；恰恰相反，软食更可能\n减少咀嚼。\n借用原文：Highly processed food tends to be softer, which makes it easier to chew and swallow\nD) 偷换概念\n录音 easier to chew and swallow 表示吞咽更容易、更快，而选项 D 'Swallow bit by bit'\n暗示慢、分小口吞，语义相反。\n借用原文：easier to chew and swallow", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "How do people generally account for differences between men and women in the amount of time spent on housework?", "options": ["A) By attributing them to current cultural influences.", "B) By attributing them to conservative thinking.", "C) In terms of new genetic findings.", "D) In terms of personal preferences."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） By attributing them to conservative thinking.\n【定位】Women continue to spend more time than men taking care of children and doing chores\naround the house. This is generally attributed to backward thinking and conservative values.\nBut new research shows that such differences could be linked to differences in the\npreferences of males and females.\n女性仍然比男性花更多时间照顾孩子和做家务。这通常被归因于落后思想和保守价值观。但\n新研究表明，这种差异可能与男性和女性的偏好差异有关。\n【信号】观点对比结构。答案句藏在转折之前：前一句 This is generally attributed to backward\nthinking and conservative values. 给出'通常看法'，But new research shows that such\ndifferences could be linked to differences in the preferences of males and females.\n引出转折后的新研究。题干问 generally，所以取 but 前的内容。\n【替换】backward thinking and conservative values ↔ conservative thinking.\n同义概括\nThis is generally attributed to ↔ By attributing them to conservative thinking.\n结构改写\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA 项 By attributing them to current cultural influences. 把录音里的 conservative\nvalues 改头换面成'当代文化影响'，原文说的是'保守'而非'当代文化'，方向相反。\nC) 未提及\nC 项 In terms of new genetic findings. 中的 genetic 在原文完全没有出现，录音只提到\npreferences，属于凭空捏造。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 In terms of personal preferences. 确实是录音原词 preferences of males and\nfemales，但它属于 but 之后的新研究发现，不是题干问的'人们通常'的看法。看到原词就\n想选是第一大陷阱。\n借用原文：preferences of males and females", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did men lean toward doing according to the new study?", "options": ["A) Sharing with women the chores they see necessary.", "B) Dividing housework that is demanding for women.", "C) Performing household tasks that they enjoy.", "D) Doing tasks that are physically challenging."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Performing household tasks that they enjoy.\n【定位】In general, both men and women took more responsibility for tasks they liked. For example,\nmen liked yard care and home maintenance more than women. They leaned toward wanting\nprimary responsibility for such tasks.\n总体而言，男性和女性都会对自己喜欢的任务承担更多责任。例如，男性比女性更喜欢庭院\n护理和房屋维修。他们倾向于想要主要负责这类任务。\n【信号】研究结论+例证。答案信号是 In general, both men and women took more\nresponsibility for tasks they liked. 这一研究总括句，随后用 men liked yard care and\nhome maintenance more than women 举例支撑。\n【替换】tasks they liked ↔ Performing household tasks that they enjoy.\n同义词替换\ntook more responsibility for ↔ Performing household tasks that they enjoy.\n动作概括\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 Sharing with women the chores they see necessary. 中的 see necessary 在原文中\n没有对应表述，属于无中生有。\nB) 偷换概念\nB 项 Dividing housework that is demanding for women. 把录音里'男女共同选择自己喜\n欢的任务'改成了'对女性来说繁重的家务'，demanding 在原文没有依据。\nD) 未提及\nD 项 Doing tasks that are physically challenging. 看似能解释 yard care / home\nmaintenance，但原文从未用 physically challenging 描述这些任务，考生若凭常识推断就\n会掉坑。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What should we keep in mind as regards any research on group differences?", "options": ["A) The large within-group differences.", "B) The great similarities across groups.", "C) The researchers' subjective biases.", "D) The noticeable overgeneralizations."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The large within-group differences.\n【定位】Keep in mind, however, that, as with any research on group differences, there were large\nwithin-group differences.\n然而，请记住，正如任何关于群体差异的研究一样，群体内部也存在很大的差异。\n【信号】提醒信号词 keep in mind。答案句以 Keep in mind, however, 开头，直接对应题干 keep\nin mind，后面跟的就是注意事项。\n【替换】large within-group differences ↔ The large within-group differences.\n原词复现\nas with any research on group differences ↔ The large within-group differences.\n题干同义对应\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nB 项 The great similarities across groups. 说'群体间高度相似'，而原文 large within-\ngroup differences 强调的是'群体内差异大'，方向相反。\n借用原文：large within-group differences\nC) 未提及\nC 项 The researchers' subjective biases. 中的 subjective biases 在原文没有提到，原文\n强调的是群体内部差异，不是研究者偏见。\nD) 过度推断\nD 项 The noticeable overgeneralizations. 是对群体差异研究可能存在的问题的推断，但\n原文只说 within-group differences 大，并未说这是 overgeneralization。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What did previous research reveal about gender differences in attitudes toward family and career?", "options": ["A) They seem to be universal.", "B) They impact people's whole life.", "C) They shape one's role in society.", "D) They appear at an early age."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They appear at an early age.\n【定位】The authors noted that previous research revealed gender differences in attitudes towards\nfamily and career. These differences appear as early as six years old.\n作者指出，先前的研究揭示了人们在家庭和职业态度上的性别差异。这些差异早在六岁时就\n出现。\n【信号】研究结论句。答案信号是 previous research revealed，直接对应题干 previous research\nreveal；后一句 These differences appear as early as six years old. 给出具体特征。\n【替换】appear as early as six years old ↔ They appear at an early age.\n概括改写\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 项 They seem to be universal. 把原文的性别差异推断为'普遍存在'，但原文只提到 six\nyears old 就出现，并未说 universal。\nB) 未提及\nB 项 They impact people's whole life. 中的 whole life 在原文没有对应表述，原文只提到\n六岁出现，未说影响一生。\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项 They shape one's role in society. 把 attitudes towards family and career 偷换成\nrole in society，扩大了概念范围。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) simply\n副词\n【词性槽】be 动词后、名词短语前，主系表已完整，只缺副词\n【依据】not simply...but rather...“不仅仅是……而是……”与后半句呼应\n后半句 but rather is influenced by a person's genetic makeup 用 but rather 转弯，前\n半句就需要一个“仅仅”来铺垫：这种敏感不仅仅是味觉问题，而是受基因影响。simply 与\nbut rather 是英文论证里的经典搭配。\n【竞争词】B) approximately “不大约是”自相矛盾\napproximately“大约”表模糊估计，与 isn't 的明确否定冲突，“不大约是一个味觉问题”语义\n别扭。\nM) separately 句中无可“分别”的多个对象\nseparately“分别地”需要至少两个并列对象（如 33 空的三种苦味），本句只谈“这种敏感”一件\n事，无处“分别”。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) opposite\n形容词\n【词性槽】The 与名词 results 之间需形容词定语\n【依据】结果与预期“相反”：预期少喝，实际多喝\n前一句刚给出预期 You'd expect that people who are particularly sensitive to the\nbitter taste of caffeine would drink less coffee（你以为敏感者会少喝），而首段事实是\nactually drink more of it（实际多喝）——结果与预期正好相反，opposite results 精准\n收口。\n【竞争词】O) targeted “有针对性的结果”无中生有\ntargeted results 暗示研究“瞄准了”某个目标对象，上下文没有任何“靶向”信息，纯属看着高级就\n选的干扰。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— targeted 词性对、词形高级，最易顶替 opposite。The\n__(27)__ results of our study 填 targeted 读起来也顺，但全段是“预期少喝 would drink\nless coffee vs 实际多喝 actually drink more of it”的反转结构，答案必须落在 opposite\n上。防坑：形容词空先找上下文的逻辑信号词（expect/actually/surprising），别被“高级\n感”带走。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) reinforcement\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 positive 后需名词作中心词\n【依据】positive reinforcement“积极强化”，解释“越敏感越爱喝”的心理机制\n前半句说喝咖啡的人 acquire a taste for or an ability to detect the bitterness of\ncaffeine（习得对苦味的喜爱），原因是咖啡因带来的习得性“积极强化”——心理学固定术\n语 positive reinforcement，正对应下段 learn to associate \"good things with it\" 的“把\n好东西与之挂钩”。\n【竞争词】H) omission “积极遗漏”语义自相矛盾\npositive omission 既不是固定搭配也不合逻辑，咖啡因“带来遗漏”无法解释人们为何学会喜欢\n苦味。\nK) prospect positive prospect 看似通顺但接不上机制\n“积极前景”语法上能站住，但 prospect 指对将来的预期，而这里解释的是“咖啡因让人上瘾般喜\n欢”的即时心理奖励机制，且 prospect 不会由咖啡因 brought out（带来）。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— positive prospect 搭配感强，最易顶替 positive\nreinforcement。the learned positive __(28)__ brought out by caffeine 填 prospect 语\n法无误，但“前景”是面向未来的预期，无法被咖啡因“带来”，也和下句 learn to associate\n\"good things with it\" 的强化机制脱节。防坑：遇到“形容词+空”疑似术语时，先想学科固\n定搭配（positive reinforcement），再用下文的解释句验证。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) heightened\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 ability 之间需形容词定语\n【依据】heightened ability“增强的味觉能力”＝首段 supersensitive 的同义改写\n首段说 people who are supersensitive to coffee's bitter taste，本段用另一种说法复\n述：拥有“增强的”品尝咖啡苦味能力的人——heightened 正是 supersensitive 的形容词化\n同义替换，科普文换词复述同一概念的套路。\n【竞争词】I) opposite “相反的能力”无从谈起\nopposite 需要一个明确的对立对象（如 27 空的 opposite results 对应“预期结果”），ability\n没有“相反的能力”这种说法；它的归宿是 27 空。\nO) targeted targeted ability 搭配生硬且偏离“敏感度”主线\ntargeted“有针对性的”多修饰 attack/therapy 等带目标对象的动作，修饰 ability 既不生造也不\n呼应全文反复强调的 sensitive。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) convince\n动词\n【词性槽】不定式 to 后接动词原形，且要能带“sb to do”\n【依据】convince sb to do sth：警告机制“促使”人吐出有害物质\nbitterness often serves as a warning mechanism to __(30)__ people to spit out\nharmful substances 讲的是苦味的生理功能——警告人别吃有毒东西，机制“说服/促使”人\n们吐出有害物质，convince people to spit out 结构与语义双贴合。\n【竞争词】C) avoid 结构不接 sb to do，且逻辑相反\navoid 后只接名词或 doing，不能说 avoid people to spit out；若理解成“避免吐出有害物\n质”，与“警告机制保命”的语义恰好拧反。它真正的归宿是 35 空。\nJ) perceive perceive 不带 sb to do，语义也不通\nperceive“察觉”的宾语是事物不是“人 + to do”，“察觉人们吐出有害物质”不是警告机制的功能。\n它真正的归宿是 32 空。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) consumption\n名词\n【词性槽】所有格 people's 后、of 短语前，需名词\n【依据】consumption of tea, coffee and alcohol＝对三样饮品的“饮用/消费”\n全文第一句就是 people who are supersensitive to coffee's bitter taste actually drink\nmore of it——研究的核心变量就是“喝多少”，consumption 正是 drink 的名词化，\nconsumption of 与三样饮品搭配天经地义。\n【竞争词】H) omission “对饮品的遗漏”与主题无关\nomission 意为“省略/遗漏”，研究的是基因如何影响人们喝什么、喝多少，与“遗漏”毫不沾边。\nK) prospect prospect of 指向未来，不指向饮用行为\nprospect“前景、可能性”接 of 时表示“……的可能”，基因影响的是现实的饮用行为而非饮品的\n“前景”。\nL) reinforcement “对饮品的强化”语义不搭\nreinforcement“强化”虽属本篇话题词（28 空用它），但“基因影响人们对茶咖酒的强化”说不\n通，强化的载体是咖啡因带来的心理效应，不是这三样饮品本身。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) perceive\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 we 后缺谓语，需现在时动词\n【依据】“我们分别感知三种苦味”，与 seem the same 构成让步转折\n整句 While all bitter flavors may seem the same, we __(32)__ the bitterness of\nBrussels sprouts(抱子甘蓝), quinine(奎宁) and caffeine __(33)__：虽然苦味看似一样，但\n我们其实是“感知/分辨”它们（而且分别地感知），perceive 正是“察觉味道差异”的专用词，\n与下句“苦味程度由基因决定”衔接。\n【竞争词】E) converted 时态冲突且“转变苦味”语义不通\n邻居动词 may seem 是现在时，we converted 突然跳过去式，时态打架；且“我们转变了三种\n物质的苦味”也不是这段在说的感知机制。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) separately\n副词\n【词性槽】句末空、主谓宾齐全，缺修饰动词的副词\n【依据】对三种并列苦味“分别地”感知，与上下句严丝合缝\n上句说 While all bitter flavors may seem the same（所有苦味看似一样），下句说 The\ndegree to which we find these flavors bitter is, in part, determined by your genes.\n（程度由基因决定），中间正是“我们分别地感知抱子甘蓝、奎宁和咖啡因的苦味”——\nseparately 把“看似一样”与“程度各异”两头都接上。\n【竞争词】B) approximately “大约”修饰列举不通\n宾语是三种清清楚楚并列的物质，没有可供“大约”估计的数量或程度，代入后句子语义垮掉。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) addition\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 In 后、逗号前，需名词构成句首衔接语\n【依据】固定搭配 in addition“此外”，承接上段并列递进\n上段刚说完一类人 People with the genes to taste the bitterness of green vegetables\nare more likely to prefer tea to coffee.，本句再补充另一类敏感人群，中间正缺“此\n外”——in addition 是英语里最常用的段落衔接短语之一。\n【竞争词】D) consumption in consumption 不成衔接短语\nconsumption 意为“消费/饮用”，填进去是“在消费中”，与上下句的并列递进关系无关。\nH) omission omission 语义不搭\nomission 是“遗漏”，“在遗漏中”既不是固定短语，也无法连接上下文。\nK) prospect in prospect 意为“在望”，逻辑不符\nin prospect 虽存在但表“可能发生、在望”，本句是在补充另一组既成事实的人群，不是预测\n未来。\nL) reinforcement in reinforcement 不构成衔接\nreinforcement“强化”填入后“在强化中”语义不通，且它真正的归宿是 28 空的\npositive reinforcement。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) addition", "B) approximately", "C) avoid", "D) consumption", "E) converted", "F) convince", "G) heightened", "H) omission", "I) opposite", "J) perceive", "K) prospect", "L) reinforcement", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) targeted"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) avoid\n动词\n【词性槽】tended to 后接动词原形\n【依据】对苦味更敏感的人倾向于“避开”咖啡，与 prefer tea to coffee 同向\n段首刚说 People with the genes to taste the bitterness of green vegetables are more\nlikely to prefer tea to coffee.（这类基因的人更爱茶不爱咖啡），本句顺着说“对奎宁和蔬\n菜苦味更敏感的人倾向于避开 avoid 咖啡”，两句同向互证；这与首段咖啡人群的“反常识多\n喝”形成对照组。\n【竞争词】J) perceive “倾向于感知咖啡”语义不通\nperceive 的对象是味道、刺激等可感知物，coffee 作为饮品是“喝”的对象；“倾向于察觉咖啡”既\n不能与上句 prefer tea to coffee 构成同向对照，也不合常识搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It is typical for dementia to worsen with the passage of time.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】dementia / worsen with the passage of time\n【定位】Dementia typically gets worse over time.\n痴呆通常会随着时间的推移而恶化。\n【改写】It is typical ↔ typically\n词性转换\nworsen with the passage of time ↔ gets worse over time\n同义短语替换\n【辨邻】 E Dementia, as defined in medical dictionaries, is a broad term that describes a\nloss of thinking ability, memory, attention, logical reasoning, and other mental\nabilities 谈的是痴呆定义，虽然话题相邻，但没有“随时间恶化”的信息。\nDementia, as defined in medical dictionaries, is a broad term that describes a loss of thinking\nability, memory, attention, logical reasoning, and other mental abilities", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "According to Knopman, the study gives the message that a healthy lifestyle is beneficial, but not that all the middle-aged need heavy exercise.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Knopman / healthy lifestyle / heavy exercise\n【定位】The message isn't that everyone needs to run marathons in middle age, Knopman said, but a\nhealthy lifestyle pays off.\nKnopman说，信息并不是说每个人到中年都需要去跑马拉松，而是健康的生活方式会有\n回报。\n【改写】According to Knopman ↔ Knopman said\n说话人锁定\nall the middle-aged need heavy exercise ↔ everyone needs to run marathons in middle age\n上位概括\na healthy lifestyle is beneficial ↔ a healthy lifestyle pays off\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 J Maintaining a healthy lifestyle in mid-life... starting in mid-life seems to offer a\nlarger benefit 虽然也是 Knopman 谈健康生活方式，但没有题干中“不必进行 heavy\nexercise”这层意思。\nMaintaining a healthy lifestyle in mid-life, decades before disease sets in, makes sense, said David\nKnopman... starting in mid-life seems to offer a larger benefit\nM That doesn't mean you have to compete in marathons 虽出现 marathons，但说\n话人是 Fargo，不是 Knopman。\nThat doesn't mean you have to compete in marathons", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The results of a new study will encourage middle-aged women to find time exercising however busy they are.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】new study / middle-aged women / find time exercising\n【定位】If this doesn't encourage you to squeeze in a workout today, nothing will: a new study from\nSweden shows that women who were highly fit in mid-life were nearly 90% less likely to get\ndementia(失智) decades later.\n如果这还不能促使你今天挤出时间锻炼，那什么都不会了：一项来自瑞典的新研究表明，中\n年时体能极高的女性晚年患失智症的几率要低近90%。\n【改写】The results of a new study ↔\na new study from Sweden shows that women who were highly fit in mid-life were nearly 90%\nless likely to get dementia(失智) decades later\n概括复现\nencourage middle-aged women to find time exercising however busy they are ↔\nIf this doesn't encourage you to squeeze in a workout today, nothing will\n同义改写\n【辨邻】 C The study, published Wednesday in the journal Neurology... the results were\nparticularly dramatic 是对研究结果的客观报道，没有 A 段那种“促使你锻炼”的劝勉\n语气。\nThe study, published Wednesday in the journal Neurology... the results were particularly dramatic\nP the time to start getting fit is now 是 Fargo 的行动建议，没有题干中的“new study\nresults”作为动因。\nthe time to start getting fit is now", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Hopefully, one can postpone or prevent dementia by exercising in their mid-life.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】postpone or prevent dementia / mid-life exercise\n【定位】it does give us hope that dementia can be prevented or delayed through exercise in one's\nmiddle age.\n它确实让我们怀有希望：痴呆可以通过中年时期的运动来预防或延缓。\n【改写】Hopefully ↔ give us hope\n名词化\npostpone or prevent dementia ↔ prevented or delayed\n同义替换\nby exercising in their mid-life ↔ through exercise in one's middle age\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 A women who were highly fit in mid-life were nearly 90% less likely to get\ndementia 只报告风险降低，未出现 hope / prevented or delayed 的表述。\nwomen who were highly fit in mid-life were nearly 90% less likely to get dementia\nK exercise helps put off or prevent Alzheimer's 只讨论运动对阿尔茨海默的预防机\n制，没有题干中的 mid-life 限定。\nexercise helps put off or prevent Alzheimer's", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "In a few years, researchers should be able to offer clearer advice on exercise and other lifestyle changes for reducing the incidence of Alzheimer's.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】a few years / clearer advice / lifestyle changes\n【定位】In the next three to five years, these studies should allow researchers to provide clearer\nrecommendations for exercise and other lifestyle modifications that might reduce Alzheimer's\nrisk, such as sleep, diet and social activities, Fargo said.\n在未来三到五年内，这些研究应能让研究人员就运动及其他生活方式调整（如睡眠、饮食和\n社交活动）提供更为明确的建议，以降低阿尔茨海默风险。\n【改写】In a few years ↔ In the next three to five years\n概括\nresearchers should be able to offer clearer advice ↔\nallow researchers to provide clearer recommendations\n同义替换\nexercise and other lifestyle changes ↔ exercise and other lifestyle modifications\n同义替换\nreducing the incidence of Alzheimer's ↔ reduce Alzheimer's risk\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 N Several lifestyle studies are getting underway soon, including one backed by\nthe Alzheimer's Association called the US POINTER study 只说明研究即将开始，\n没有“提供 clearer recommendations”的表述。\nSeveral lifestyle studies are getting underway soon, including one backed by the Alzheimer's\nAssociation called the US POINTER study\nM The literature has not yet settled on an amount or type of exercise 讨论的是当前\n文献局限，与题干的未来展望不同。\nThe literature has not yet settled on an amount or type of exercise", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Dementia refers to a loss of multiple mental abilities such as memory and logical reasoning.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Dementia / loss of multiple mental abilities / memory and logical reasoning\n【定位】Dementia, as defined in medical dictionaries, is a broad term that describes a loss of thinking\nability, memory, attention, logical reasoning, and other mental abilities.\n正如医学词典所定义的，痴呆是一个广义术语，描述的是思考能力、记忆力、注意力、逻辑\n推理及其他心智能力的丧失。\n【改写】Dementia refers to ↔\nDementia, as defined in medical dictionaries, is a broad term that describes\n定义句式转换\na loss of multiple mental abilities ↔\na loss of thinking ability, memory, attention, logical reasoning, and other mental abilities\n上位概括\nsuch as memory and logical reasoning ↔ memory, attention, logical reasoning\n具体列举复现\n【辨邻】 F Dementia typically gets worse over time 讨论的是病程，而非题干所问的定义。\nDementia typically gets worse over time\nG Dementia is typically associated with declines in cognitive function 只提到\ncognitive function，没有 memory / logical reasoning 等列举。\nDementia is typically associated with declines in cognitive function", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "One researcher believes that exercise may keep the brain well supplied with blood.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】exercise / brain / well supplied with blood\n【定位】it's likely that exercise maintains good blood flow to the brain.\n运动很可能维持大脑良好的血液供应。\n【改写】One researcher ↔ Knopman said\n指代锁定\nexercise may keep the brain well supplied with blood ↔\nexercise maintains good blood flow to the brain\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 J starting in mid-life seems to offer a larger benefit 是 Knopman 关于运动时机的\n主张，与脑部供血无关。\nstarting in mid-life seems to offer a larger benefit\nH dementia can be prevented or delayed through exercise in one's middle age 谈\n的是运动效果，没有 blood flow 的机制内容。\ndementia can be prevented or delayed through exercise in one's middle age", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Medical literature has not concluded as to what exercise or how much exercise is crucial, although it generally agrees that people should do aerobic exercise.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Medical literature / what exercise / how much exercise / aerobic exercise\n【定位】The literature has not yet settled on an amount or type of exercise that is going to be key,\nalthough the bulk of literature has suggested that aerobic(有氧的) exercise is what you need\nto be doing,\n文献尚未就能起关键作用的运动量或运动类型达成共识，尽管大部分文献认为你应该进行有\n氧运动。\n【改写】Medical literature has not concluded ↔ The literature has not yet settled\n同义替换\nas to what exercise or how much exercise is crucial ↔\non an amount or type of exercise that is going to be key\n同义替换\nalthough it generally agrees that people should do aerobic exercise ↔\nalthough the bulk of literature has suggested that aerobic exercise is what you need to be doing\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 L The message isn't that everyone needs to run marathons in middle age,\nKnopman said, but a healthy lifestyle pays off 是 Knopman 的观点，与 Medical\nliterature 的 consensus 无关。\nThe message isn't that everyone needs to run marathons in middle age, Knopman said, but a healthy\nlifestyle pays off\nO In the next three to five years, these studies should allow researchers to\nprovide clearer recommendations 讲未来展望，不是当前文献状态。\nIn the next three to five years, these studies should allow researchers to provide\nclearer recommendations", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "It has long been common knowledge that exercise correlates with reduced risk of dementia.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】long been common knowledge / exercise / reduced risk of dementia\n【定位】the findings aren't a surprise—it's long been known there's a correlation between exercise and\ndecreased dementia risk\n这些发现并不令人惊讶——长期以来人们都知道运动与痴呆风险降低之间存在相关性。\n【改写】It has long been common knowledge ↔ it's long been known\n同义替换\nthat exercise correlates with reduced risk of dementia ↔\nthere's a correlation between exercise and decreased dementia risk\n句式转换\n【辨邻】 A women who were highly fit in mid-life were nearly 90% less likely to get\ndementia 是瑞典新研究的具体结果，而非“早已知道”的 common knowledge。\nwomen who were highly fit in mid-life were nearly 90% less likely to get dementia\nH it does give us hope that dementia can be prevented or delayed through\nexercise in one's middle age 强调研究带来的 hope，不是 long been known。\nit does give us hope that dementia can be prevented or delayed through exercise in one's middle age", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Activities helpful in the prevention of Alzheimer's also prevent heart disease.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Activities / prevention of Alzheimer's / heart disease\n【定位】And the same activities that help prevent Alzheimer's—including avoiding smoking, getting\nadequate exercise and sleep and eating a healthy diet—also prevent heart disease, he said,\nmaking them even more worthwhile.\n他说，同样有助于预防阿尔茨海默的活动——包括不吸烟、充足运动与睡眠、健康饮食——\n也能预防心脏病，这使它们更有价值。\n【改写】Activities helpful in the prevention of Alzheimer's ↔\nthe same activities that help prevent Alzheimer's\n名词化\nalso prevent heart disease ↔ also prevent heart disease\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 K exercise helps put off or prevent Alzheimer's 讨论运动对阿尔茨海默的预防机制，\n没有 heart disease。\nexercise helps put off or prevent Alzheimer's\nJ Maintaining a healthy lifestyle in mid-life... makes sense 讲中年健康生活方式合\n理，但未出现 prevent Alzheimer's 与 prevent heart disease 的对应。\nMaintaining a healthy lifestyle in mid-life, decades before disease sets in, makes sense", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about people who suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon?", "options": ["A) They systematically underestimate their chances of success.", "B) They tend to attribute their achievements to good fortune.", "C) They seem to live in constant fear of being deceived.", "D) They generally rely on luck and chance for success."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about people who suffer from the so-called\nImpostor Phenomenon? 是问'关于某类人我们能了解到什么'，答案应在文中找对该类人的\n直接描述。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about people who suffer from the so-called\nImpostor Phenomenon? 是主谓宾结构的特殊疑问句；主干为 What do we learn，\nabout 短语限定讨论对象，who 定语从句修饰 people，整体无否定、比较等陷阱。\n【定位】They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance.\n他们认为任何成功都归因于外部环境，或者仅仅是运气和机遇。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项说 systematically underestimate their chances of success，但原文是\nsystematically underestimate themselves and their own performance，低估的对\n象从'自己和自身表现'被偷换为'成功几率'，语义不一致。\nB) ✓ 正确\nany success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance ↔\ntend to attribute their achievements to good fortune\ntend to attribute their achievements to good fortune 准确对应 any success is due\nto... luck and chance，只是把'成功归因于运气'换了说法。\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 live in constant fear of being deceived 利用了原文 live in constant fear 和\ndeception 两个词，但原文是害怕'自己的欺骗被揭穿'，不是害怕'被别人欺骗'。\nD) 过度推断\n选项 generally rely on luck and chance for success 说患者'依靠'运气获得成功，但原\n文 any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance 只是说他\n们'认为'成功归因于运气，并非真的去依靠运气。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do people with a healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt tend to do?", "options": ["A) Credit all their successes to hard work.", "B) Strive hard to perfect their skills.", "C) Refrain from acting on impulse.", "D) Deliver a better performance."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do people with a healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt tend to do? 询问\n具备某特征的人群 tend to do 什么，属于对文中具体行为信息的提取。\n【拆句】What do people with a healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt tend to do? 中\nwith 短语作后置定语修饰 people，tend to do 询问习惯性结果，句子平直，无复杂从句。\n【定位】A healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively.\n适度的反思和自我怀疑能够防止一个人冲动行事。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 Credit all their successes to hard work 说健康反思者把所有成功归功于努力，但\n原文 there are people who are permanently plagued by... self-doubt 之后说的是 IP\n患者不把成功归因于自身 skill or hard work，并未说健康反思者就会归因于努力。\nB) 无中生有\n选项 Strive hard to perfect their skills 在原文关于健康反思者的描述中没有对应信息，\n属于凭空添加。\nC) ✓ 正确\nprotect a person from acting impulsively ↔ Refrain from acting on impulse\nRefrain from acting on impulse 与 protect a person from acting impulsively 语义\n等值，只是从'被保护不冲动'转换为'主动克制不冲动'。\nD) 答非所问\n选项 Deliver a better performance 暗示健康反思者会表现更好，但原文 despite\ndelivering a good performance 是修饰 IP 患者的，与健康反思者无比较关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do Impostor Phenomenon sufferers often do according to psychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg?", "options": ["A) Attribute their accomplishments to others.", "B) Deny their actual measured intelligence.", "C) Claim to have confidence in their own abilities.", "D) Mark down their objectively rated performance."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do Impostor Phenomenon sufferers often do according to psychologists from\nMartin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg? 问的是某研究发现中患者'经常做什么'，答\n案应出自研究结论句。\n【拆句】What do Impostor Phenomenon sufferers often do according to psychologists from\nMartin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg? 主干为 What do sufferers do，according\nto 引出信息来源，句子结构简单，无否定比较。\n【定位】people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively\nmeasured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and\nchance, but not to their own abilities.\n有表现出冒名顶替现象倾向的人会贬低他们客观测得的表现，并把积极结果归因于运气等外\n部原因，而非他们自己的能力。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项 Attribute their accomplishments to others 说患者把成就归因于'他人'，但原文\nattribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance 说的是'运气等\n外部原因'，external causes 不等于 others。\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 Deny their actual measured intelligence 说患者否认实际测得的智力，但原文\nthe self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual\nmeasured intelligence or performance 只是说两者无关，并未说患者否认智力。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 Claim to have confidence in their own abilities 说患者声称对自己能力有信心，\n但原文明确说患者把成功归因于外部原因 'but not to their own abilities'，说明他们并\n不信任自己的能力。\nD) ✓ 正确\ndevalue their objectively measured performance ↔\nMark down their objectively rated performance\nMark down their objectively rated performance 几乎是 devalue their objectively\nmeasured performance 的同义改写，只是换了动词和形容词。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What happens when a person permanently underestimates their own abilities?", "options": ["A) They will focus their attention on external factors.", "B) They will be fearful of potential consequences.", "C) They will count on good fortune for success.", "D) They will keep improving their own skills."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What happens when a person permanently underestimates their own abilities? 询问\n某种情况下会发生什么，答案在原文的条件-结果句中。\n【拆句】What happens when a person permanently underestimates their own abilities? 中\nwhen 引导时间/条件状语从句，主句省略了 happen 的实体主语，整体为简单因果结构。\n【定位】A permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this\nsupposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the\nprice for this.\n长期低估自己的能力常常伴随着一种恐惧：这种所谓的智力欺骗迟早会被揭穿，并且他们会\n为此付出代价。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n选项 focus their attention on external factors 描述的是患者对成功的外部归因，属于\n第 46、48 题的内容，不是长期低估能力带来的结果。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they\nwill pay the price for this\n↔ be fearful of potential consequences\nbe fearful of potential consequences 把原文 '害怕欺骗被揭穿、害怕付出代价' 概括\n为'害怕潜在后果'，符合同义概括的替换方式。\nC) 过度推断\n选项 count on good fortune for success 说患者'指望'好运获得成功，但原文只是说他\n们认为成功归因于好运，并非真的把成功寄托于好运。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 keep improving their own skills 在文中没有对应信息，属于用常识捏造的积极\n结果。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What did US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes say about Impostor Phenomenon sufferers?", "options": ["A) They are more liable to have depression.", "B) They seldom realise their potential fully.", "C) They will develop mental illnesses.", "D) They will pay higher prices one day."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What did US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes say about Impostor\nPhenomenon sufferers? 问特定人物对患者的说法，属于对文中观点句的提取。\n【拆句】What did US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes say about Impostor\nPhenomenon sufferers? 是过去时特殊疑问句，say about 后接对象，结构简单；注意\ndid 提示事件发生在过去。\n【定位】However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression.\n然而，患有冒名顶替现象的人表现出更高的抑郁易感性。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\npeople who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression ↔\nThey are more liable to have depression\nThey are more liable to have depression 准确对应 people who suffer from it show\na higher vulnerability to depression，把'易感性高'换成'更容易患上'。\nB) 过度推断\n选项 They seldom realise their potential fully 说患者很少充分发挥潜力，原文只提到\n他们低估自己和害怕被揭穿，没有明确说他们潜力发挥不足。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 They will develop mental illnesses 说患者会发展成精神疾病，但原文 The\nImpostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness 明确说它不是精神疾病。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 They will pay higher prices one day 把原文 they will pay the price for this 中\n的 the price 改成了 higher prices，添加了比较含义，改变了原意。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "In what way is music important?", "options": ["A) It is able to create novel and extraordinary emotions.", "B) It has the power to unite people throughout the world.", "C) It allows different cultural groups to sing in harmony.", "D) It is the only language favored by all peoples of the world."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】In what way is music important? 用 In what way 询问音乐重要性的具体表现，属于对文\n中具体信息的考查。\n【拆句】In what way is music important? 为简单疑问句，主干是 music is important，In what\nway 询问方式或方面，无否定、比较等陷阱。\n【定位】It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace\nand harmony.\n音乐在全球范围内都极为重要，并且有可能将人们凝聚在一起，实现和平与和谐。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项 It is able to create novel and extraordinary emotions 把原文 This can create\nextraordinary results 中的 extraordinary results 偷换为 extraordinary emotions，\n并新增 novel，改变了原文对象。\nB) ✓ 正确\nhas the potential to bring people together ↔ has the power to unite people\nacross the globe ↔ throughout the world\n原文说音乐有潜力将全球人民凝聚在和平与和谐中，It has the power to unite people\nthroughout the world 用 power 替换 potential，unite 替换 bring together，\nthroughout the world 替换 across the globe，整体语义完全一致。\nC) 过度推断\n原文 bring people together in peace and harmony 中的 harmony 是比喻性的和平\n和谐，选项 It allows different cultural groups to sing in harmony 把它具体化为 '唱\n歌和谐'，并添加 sing，属于过度具体化。\nD) 过度推断\n原文 Music is a universal language 只说音乐是通用语言，并未说它是 the only\nlanguage favored by all peoples of the world，only 与 favored 均无出处。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why does the author like to see musicians from different cultures perform together?", "options": ["A) Their musical experience can be compared.", "B) A novel language can be created.", "C) Their unique skills can be displayed.", "D) Fantastic results can be achieved."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the author like to see musicians from different cultures perform together?\n用 Why 询问作者喜欢某现象的原因，属于因果类细节题。\n【拆句】Why does the author like to see musicians from different cultures perform together?\n为 why 引导的特殊疑问句，主干是 the author likes to see musicians perform\ntogether，无复杂从句。\n【定位】One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come\ntogether and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the\ngroup, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results.\n我在音乐中最喜欢的经历之一，就是看到来自不同文化的音乐家聚到一起、作为一个团体表\n演。当每种文化都为这个团体带来自己独特的特质时，一种全新的声音就产生了。这能创造\n出非凡的成果。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项 Their musical experience can be compared 提出 '比较经历'，但原文 P1 只描述\n作者喜欢观看这种表演，未涉及比较。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 an entirely new sound is achieved 说的是产生全新声音，选项 A novel\nlanguage can be created 把 sound 偷换为 language。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group 强调文化特质被带入\n团体以创造新声音，选项 Their unique skills can be displayed 把 qualities 窄化为\nskills，并把 brings 改为 displayed，重心从 '共同创造' 偏移为 '展示个人技能'。\nD) ✓ 正确\nThis can create extraordinary results. ↔ Fantastic results can be achieved.\nan entirely new sound is achieved ↔ Fantastic results can be achieved.\n作者喜欢跨文化合作的原因在 This can create extraordinary results，Fantastic\nresults can be achieved 用被动语态和 fantastic 同义改写 create extraordinary\nresults，准确回答原因。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the passage say about the pentatonic scale?", "options": ["A) It is present in music all around the world.", "B) It is helpful to understanding music.", "C) It is the basis for musicians' creation.", "D) It is a medium for ordinary people to relate."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about the pentatonic scale? 直接问文中关于某对象的具体\n说法，属于典型细节题。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about the pentatonic scale? 为 what 引导的特殊疑问句，\nabout 的宾语 pentatonic scale 是定位核心，结构简单。\n【定位】This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans,\nand Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock.\n这种音阶可以在世界各地的音乐中找到，包括美洲原住民、非洲人和亚洲人的音乐。它也存\n在于流行音乐、蓝调、爵士乐和摇滚乐中。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThis scale can be found in music everywhere ↔ It is present in music all around the world.\nincluding that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians ↔\nIt is present in music all around the world.\n原文 This scale can be found in music everywhere 与 It is present in music all\naround the world 语义完全等值，仅表达方式不同。\nB) 过度推断\n原文 musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can\nrelate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other 说的是音乐\n家相互理解，选项 It is helpful to understanding music 把对象扩大为 '理解音乐'，范\n围过宽。\nC) 过度推断\n原文 a medium through which they can relate 说五声音阶是交流的媒介，选项 It is\nthe basis for musicians' creation 把 medium 提升为 basis for creation，夸大了其\n地位。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can\nrelate 的主体是 musicians，选项 It is a medium for ordinary people to relate 把主\n体换成 ordinary people。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is the fundamental role of music in people's life?", "options": ["A) To bring them together.", "B) To show their identity.", "C) To express their emotions.", "D) To keep them entertained."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the fundamental role of music in people's life? 用 What is the ... role 询问音\n乐的根本作用，属于细节题。\n【拆句】What is the fundamental role of music in people's life? 为 what 引导的特殊疑问句，of\nmusic in people's life 是后置定语，修饰 role，结构平铺直叙。\n【定位】Music essentially serves to express or change emotions.\n音乐本质上用于表达或改变情绪。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n原文 has the potential to bring people together 与 it has the power to unite us 确\n实是音乐的作用，但题干问的是 fundamental role，答案应是 essentially serves to\nexpress or change emotions，A 项答非所问。\nB) 无中生有\n选项 To show their identity 提出 '展示身份'，但原文从未提到 identity 或相关概念。\nC) ✓ 正确\nMusic essentially serves to express or change emotions ↔ To express their emotions.\n原文 Music essentially serves to express or change emotions 中，express or\nchange emotions 是音乐的本质功能，To express their emotions 提取了其中\nexpress emotions 这一核心，对应 fundamental role。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文 Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun 提到娱乐是音乐\n的用途之一，但选项 To keep them entertained 把它提升为根本作用，忽略了 express\nor change emotions 的核心地位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "In what aspect is music similar throughout the world?", "options": ["A) The way people respond to it.", "B) The role it plays in society.", "C) The joy it brings to people.", "D) The way it is made use of."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】In what aspect is music similar throughout the world? 用 In what aspect 询问全球音\n乐相似的具体方面，属于细节题。\n【拆句】In what aspect is music similar throughout the world? 为 in what aspect 引导的特殊\n疑问句，主干是 music is similar，throughout the world 限定范围，无复杂结构。\n【定位】Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that\nthe purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Any way you have used music, it\nis almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way.\n尽管每种文化都有自己独特的音乐风格和处理方式，但你会发现音乐背后的目的总体上是\n十分相似的。你使用音乐的任何方式，几乎可以肯定其他文化也以完全相同的方式使用过\n音乐。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 every other culture has used music in the very same way 说的是 '使用' 音乐的\n方式相同，选项 The way people respond to it 把它改为 '回应' 音乐的方式，动作性质\n改变。\nB) 以偏概全\n原文 the purposes behind music are very similar across the board 强调使用目的/\n方式相似，选项 The role it plays in society 把它扩大为 '社会角色'，范围过宽且未突出\n'方式'。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文 Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun 提到 joy 是娱\n乐用途的一部分，选项 The joy it brings to people 把它提升为音乐全球相似性的方\n面，范围过窄。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe purposes behind music are very similar across the board ↔ The way it is made use of.\nevery other culture has used music in the very same way ↔ The way it is made use of.\n原文指出不同文化音乐的目的和使用方式非常相似，The way it is made use of 准确概\n括了 purposes behind music 与 used music in the same way 两者。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
